############################################################
# FINDINGS — cache-friendly SLO scenario
############################################################
#  HIT_RATIO / mode   lookup p99   gate   replicas   hits/misses
#  0.995   steady     11.5 ms      PASS   2 (none)   10752/49
#  0.99    steady     70.4 ms      PASS   2 (none)   10693/108
#  0.90    steady     708.5 ms     FAIL   2 (none)    9727/1074
#  0.995   SPIKE60→600 191 ms      PASS   2 (none)   47237/233
#  (hit_duration median ~0.6–1.8ms; miss_duration ~705ms — model confirmed)
#
# CONCLUSIONS:
#  1. p99<200ms is MET on the cache-friendly workload (11.5ms @99.5%, 70ms @99%) —
#     DEMONSTRATED, not asserted. Closes the REPORT #4 gap.
#  2. Crossover exactly as predicted: at 90% hits, p90=74ms (a hit) but p95/p99≈705ms
#     (the 700ms miss floor surfaces in the top ~10%). => strict p99<200 needs ≥~99% hits
#     OR bounded misses (validates the layered fix, "With another week" #7).
#  3. NO scaling in ANY run (steady 2 replicas), incl. the 10× spike — cache-friendly load
#     has low concurrency, so the autoscaler correctly does NOT fire (specificity). Contrast:
#     the miss-heavy storm scales 2→7 but can't meet the SLO (store floor).
#  4. Cache-friendly SPIKE is absorbed WITHOUT scaling, p99 191ms < 200 — but near the edge
#     (hit p99 rose to ~127ms under 600/s; 229 dropped iters ~0.5%): absorbed, not free.
############################################################

############################################################
# TAIL ROOT-CAUSE (spike run) — why max=1.38s despite no saturation
############################################################
# Prometheus over the spike window (~600/s burst, 2 replicas):
#   in-flight     : peak 6/pod   (< 10 scale threshold -> autoscaler correctly did NOT fire)
#   CPU           : peak 242m/pod (> 200m request, < 500m limit; ~7% CFS-throttled — modest)
#   Node event-loop lag p99 : 258ms / 102ms per pod
#   Node event-loop lag MAX : 840ms / 1316ms per pod   <-- explains overall max 1.38s
#
# CAUSE: NOT pool saturation, NOT CPU-limit throttling, NOT scaling lag. It's Node's
#   SINGLE-THREADED EVENT LOOP stalling under the burst: at ~300/s per pod the synchronous
#   per-request work (JSON parse -> zod validate -> serialize) + GC pauses stall the loop up
#   to ~1.3s, and everything queued behind the stall — including sub-ms cache HITS — inherits
#   that latency (hit max 600ms on a Redis GET).
#
# WHY REACTIVE SCALING CAN'T CATCH IT: both the concurrency signal (6/pod) AND CPU (242m,
#   ~48% of limit) stay UNDER threshold, so neither fires — yet the tail degrades. This is a
#   sub-threshold, bursty phenomenon.
#
# => Confirms: cache holds the SLO in STEADY STATE; under a spike it prevents *saturation*
#    but the TAIL degrades. Meeting p99<200ms *during spikes* needs the layered fix (#7):
#    predictive pre-scaling + headroom (spread the burst across more event loops),
#    per-pod load-shedding, and bounded misses (the 700ms+ half of the tail).
############################################################
